const sourceValue = source('agentsEval/scientific-consistency/task/input.md','# AURORA-24 internal integrated efficacy report\n\n## Document control and study objective\n\nThis internal report summarizes the locked AURORA-24 study database for the sponsor\'s report-control meeting. The\nstudy compared investigational therapy AX-17 with placebo in adults with persistent inflammatory symptoms. The report\nis not a clinical recommendation. Its purpose is to reconcile the executive summary, analysis definitions, primary\nresults, subgroup exploration, tables, and conclusion before external drafting begins.\n\nThe primary objective was to estimate the week-24 difference between AX-17 and placebo in responder rate. A responder\nhad at least a 30% improvement from the subject\'s own baseline symptom score and no prohibited rescue treatment during\nthe week-24 assessment window. The protocol described a treatment-policy estimand for rescue treatment and treatment\ndiscontinuation. The primary analysis used the modified intention-to-treat population and a model-adjusted response\nrate difference expressed in percentage points.\n\n## Executive summary\n\nThe executive summary states that AX-17 improved the primary responder outcome by 18% relative to placebo at week 24\nin the modified intention-to-treat population. It labels the value “primary treatment benefit” and says that the\nestimate is model adjusted. No baseline response rate or formula for a relative reduction appears in this section.\n\nThe same summary reports a 14.3 percentage-point adjusted difference in week-24 responder rate for AX-17 versus\nplacebo in the modified intention-to-treat population. It attributes this value to the treatment-policy estimand and\nstates that it is rounded to one decimal place. The summary also notes that the per-protocol sensitivity analysis\nshowed a 17.8 percentage-point difference, but it clearly labels that result as a sensitivity estimate in a different\nanalysis population.\n\nFor symptom severity, the summary says the mean improvement from each group\'s own baseline was 12.0 points with AX-17\nand 8.1 points with placebo at week 12. Those are within-group mean changes, not a between-group response-rate metric.\nThe summary does not describe either change score as the primary treatment benefit.\n\n## Analysis populations and estimands\n\nThe randomized population contained 428 participants: 215 assigned to AX-17 and 213 assigned to placebo. The safety\npopulation contained all 425 participants who received at least one dose. The modified intention-to-treat population\ncontained 418 treated participants who had a baseline symptom score and at least one post-baseline assessment: 211 in\nthe AX-17 arm and 207 in the placebo arm.\n\nThe per-protocol population excluded major eligibility deviations, prohibited concomitant medication, and absence of\nthe week-24 assessment. It contained 366 participants. Because these exclusions occur after randomization, values from\nthe per-protocol population are not interchangeable with modified intention-to-treat estimates. The subgroup of\nparticipants younger than 50 years contained 184 modified intention-to-treat participants and was exploratory.\n\nThe primary estimand was the treatment-policy difference in week-24 responder rate. Rescue use and discontinuation\nwere handled through multiple imputation under the treatment-policy strategy. The estimand was evaluated in the\nmodified intention-to-treat population. A hypothetical no-rescue estimand was used only in sensitivity analysis and\nmust not be treated as the primary estimand.\n\n## Statistical methods\n\nThe primary analysis estimated adjusted responder rates using a generalized linear model with treatment, region, and\nbaseline severity stratum. The reported treatment contrast was AX-17 minus placebo, expressed as an absolute\npercentage-point difference. Model-adjusted arm rates and their difference were calculated from marginal predictions.\nThe report prespecified one-decimal-place presentation in narrative sections, while the main table retained three\ndecimal places as proportions.\n\nFor the primary metric, a tabulated difference of 0.143 is therefore the same value as a narrative difference of 14.3\npercentage points. It is not equivalent to a 14.3% relative improvement. A relative improvement would require an\nexplicit reference rate and a ratio-based formula, neither of which defines the primary metric.\n\nThe per-protocol sensitivity analysis used the same adjusted response-rate-difference metric and week-24 horizon but a\ndifferent analysis population. The no-rescue sensitivity analysis used the hypothetical estimand. The week-12 symptom\nscore analysis used arithmetic mean change from each group\'s own baseline. These analyses answer different questions\nand should not be numerically reconciled with the primary response-rate difference.\n\nThe report considers a one-decimal narrative value consistent with a three-decimal proportion when the normalized\nproportion lies within the rounding interval. Confidence intervals are reported separately from point estimates and\nare not substituted for them.\n\n## Primary efficacy results\n\nIn the modified intention-to-treat population at week 24, the model-adjusted responder rate was 0.614 for AX-17 and\n0.471 for placebo. The adjusted absolute difference was 0.143, corresponding to 14.3 percentage points. The two-sided\n95% confidence interval for the absolute difference was 0.051 to 0.235. This analysis used the treatment-policy\nestimand described above.\n\nA draft sentence remaining in the results narrative states that the adjusted absolute difference was 12.0 percentage\npoints for the same week-24 modified intention-to-treat treatment-policy analysis. The sentence cites the primary\nmodel and does not name a different baseline, population, aggregation, horizon, estimand, or rounding rule. No table\nor derivation supports 12.0 percentage points for this analysis target.\n\nThe unadjusted observed-case responder rates were 0.602 and 0.478, an unadjusted difference of 12.4 percentage points.\nThis observed-case estimate has a different aggregation and missing-data treatment from the adjusted primary result.\nIt is presented as descriptive and is not the primary adjusted estimate.\n\n## Sensitivity and subgroup results\n\nThe per-protocol week-24 adjusted responder-rate difference was 17.8 percentage points for AX-17 minus placebo. The\npopulation consisted only of the 366 per-protocol participants. This value is larger than the primary 14.3\npercentage-point estimate, but the population definition differs and the report labels the analysis as sensitivity.\n\nUnder the hypothetical no-rescue estimand in the modified intention-to-treat population, the adjusted week-24\nresponder-rate difference was 15.6 percentage points. The analysis changes the estimand rather than the metric or\npopulation. It therefore must not be treated as a contradictory restatement of the treatment-policy result.\n\nAmong participants younger than 50 years, the exploratory adjusted week-24 responder-rate difference was 21.1\npercentage points. The corresponding value among participants aged 50 years or older was 9.2 percentage points.\nThese subgroup results concern narrower populations and were not powered for a primary inference.\n\nAt week 12, mean symptom-score change from each group\'s own baseline was -12.0 points for AX-17 and -8.1 points for\nplacebo. A separate adjusted least-squares mean contrast was -3.6 score points. Neither the within-group change nor the\nleast-squares mean contrast is a week-24 responder-rate difference.\n\n## Table 7. Primary adjusted responder analysis\n\nTable 7 is titled “Modified intention-to-treat treatment-policy responder analysis at week 24.” Its rows report AX-17\nadjusted response proportion 0.614, placebo adjusted response proportion 0.471, and AX-17-minus-placebo adjusted\ndifference 0.143. The table footnote states that proportions use marginal model predictions and that the difference\nis an absolute contrast. The confidence interval row reports 0.051 to 0.235.\n\nThe table\'s population footnote defines modified intention-to-treat as all dosed randomized participants with baseline\nand post-baseline data. The estimand footnote identifies treatment policy for rescue and discontinuation. The display\nretains three decimal places; it does not use relative percent notation.\n\n## Table 8. Sensitivity analyses\n\nTable 8 reports 0.178 for the per-protocol adjusted absolute response-rate difference at week 24 and 0.156 for the\nhypothetical no-rescue estimand in the modified intention-to-treat population. The caption identifies both rows as\nsensitivity analyses. A third row reports the unadjusted observed-case difference of 0.124 and labels it descriptive.\n\nThese values share some dimensions with the primary analysis but not all of them. Table 8 does not purport to replace\nthe primary table and does not provide support for the draft 12.0 percentage-point primary sentence.\n\n## Data-quality appendix\n\nThe data-management reconciliation found no duplicate randomized identifiers. Three treated participants lacked any\npost-baseline symptom assessment and four lacked the baseline score required by the modified intention-to-treat\ndefinition. These facts explain the difference between randomized and modified intention-to-treat counts. They do not\nchange the analysis target after database lock.\n\nThe report-control extraction covered the executive summary, analysis-population definitions, estimand definition,\nstatistical methods, primary results, sensitivity results, subgroup section, Table 7, Table 8, and this appendix for\nquantitative efficacy claims. Coverage is closed for primary and sensitivity efficacy claims within these sections.\nSafety narratives and exploratory pharmacokinetic listings were outside this audit scope.\n\nOne editorial note asks whether “18% relative to placebo” in the executive summary was intended as a relative metric\nor as informal wording for the 14.3 percentage-point primary difference. The source does not resolve that question.\nBoth interpretations must remain visible: as written, the metric is relative percent and is not comparable with the\nabsolute primary difference; if an editor later changes the metric, that new revision must be analyzed separately.\n\n## Integrated conclusion\n\nThe integrated conclusion states that the primary week-24 modified intention-to-treat treatment-policy analysis showed\nan adjusted absolute responder-rate difference of 14.3 percentage points for AX-17 versus placebo. It cites Table 7\nand uses the same metric, baseline comparator, population, aggregation, horizon, and estimand as the primary result.\n\nThe conclusion separately notes that sensitivity estimates ranged from 15.6 to 17.8 percentage points under different\nestimands or populations. It does not present those values as alternate calculations of the primary analysis. It also\ndescribes the week-12 symptom-score findings as supportive but distinct.\n\nBefore external release, the report-control team must resolve the unsupported draft statement of 12.0 percentage\npoints for the primary analysis. The team must also clarify or remove the executive-summary phrase “18% relative to\nplacebo,” because it names a different metric and lacks the baseline required to reconstruct a relative effect. These\neditorial actions do not change the locked Table 7 value of 0.143 or its normalized presentation as 14.3 percentage\npoints.\n\n## Report-control disposition log\n\nThe statistical lead confirmed that Table 7 is the locked source for the primary point estimate. The lead classified\nthe 12.0 percentage-point primary sentence as a transcription defect, not an alternative model result. The report\nowner retained the sentence in this internal revision so the automated control could demonstrate that a genuine\ncross-section conflict is detected before editorial correction. The owner also retained the 18% relative phrase\nbecause its metric ambiguity requires a separate editorial decision rather than numeric reconciliation.\n\nThe quality reviewer recorded three expected outcomes. First, 0.143 in Table 7, 14.3 percentage points in the primary\nresults, 14.3 percentage points in the executive summary, and 14.3 percentage points in the integrated conclusion\nshould normalize to a consistent primary value. Second, the 12.0 percentage-point sentence has the same analysis\ndimensions and should be reported as a numeric conflict. Third, 17.8 in the per-protocol sensitivity analysis, 15.6\nunder the hypothetical estimand, 12.4 for the unadjusted observed-case analysis, and subgroup estimates should not be\ncompared as contradictory primary values because at least one critical dimension differs.\n\nThe reviewer did not authorize conversion of the 18% relative phrase into an absolute percentage-point difference.\nThere is no explicit reference risk or calculation supporting that conversion. The phrase therefore remains a\ndifferent-metric comparison and the audit should classify it as non-applicable for direct numeric conflict review,\nwhile retaining a clarification item for human follow-up.\n\nAll sections named in the data-quality appendix were available to the report-control process in this revision. The\ncoverage declaration applies only to efficacy claim support in those sections. It does not assert complete extraction\nof safety events, pharmacokinetic observations, protocol deviations unrelated to analysis-set membership, or external\nmedical evidence. A later source revision must receive a new task identity and new spans even if most prose remains\nunchanged.\n','working');
